  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 200 PM Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Scattered showers today with localized areas of \nheavy rainfall possible. Lesser storm coverage on Sunday, \nthen turning drier for much of the week ahead.\n\nEarly afternoon water vapor imagery reveals a weak baggy trough \naloft with broad southwesterly flow across the region. Low and mid \nlevel WV channels depicts a deeper plume of moisture along and east \nof US-1 with markedly drier air to the west. PW's this morning were \nsampled at around 1.4\" per 12Z KGSO sounding while nearly 2.3\" at \nKMHX. At the surface a weak stationary front remains stretched \nacross NC from east to west (generally along US-64). This was the \nfocal area for isolated shower and storm development this morning as \na series of weak waves rode along the front.\n\nFor the remainder of today, weak forcing for ascent will drift \nnorthward from SC into NC which should be enough to kick off \nadditional showers along and south of the stationary front which we \nare already seeing. Given the abundance of cloud cover from this \nmorning, surface based and mixed layer instability will be lacking \n(still around 1500 J/KG but a far cry from the 2000-3000 J/KG that \nhad been readily available the last few days). This, combined with \nweak effective shear values less than 20kts, should keep most storms \nbelow severe thresholds although a few strong gusts can't be ruled \nout with the deepest storms. Thus the primary threat from slow \nmoving storms remains periods of heavy rain and isolated flooding. \nGiven meager instability and a gradual eastward progression of the \nstrongest forcing, shower/storm activity should diminish greatly \nwith the loss of daytime heating. PoPs today are highest south and \neast of the Triangle, with lesser values across the western \nPiedmont. A few showers could linger near the NC/SC border late \ntonight as the baggy upper trough slides through the area but these \nshould be fairly isolated in nature. \n\nLooking ahead to Sunday, even fewer ingredients will be in place for \nshower/storm development. The surface boundary will be all but \nwashed out by Sunday morning and PW's will be even lower area-wide, \nalthough a PW gradient will remain in place across the Coastal Plain \nand a few showers can't be ruled out along and east of I-95 Sunday \nafternoon. Meanwhile, mid level height rises which begin on Sunday \nwill continue through much of the week at strong ridging sets up \nover much of the Southeast. This will result in greatly diminished \nstorm chances as we get into the week with very little/if any storm \nactivity until at least Thursday or Friday.  \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... A hot start to September across central NC.\n\nAloft, the ~595 dam mid-level anticyclone will build eastward into \nand settle over the region through at least mid-week. Disturbances \nin the northern stream may help push the ridge south and west toward \nthe end of the week, but uncertainty remains in that regard. At the \nsurface, a weak pressure trough may remain in place across the area \nthrough, while Bermuda high pressure ridges wwd across the region. \nExpect a sly component to the wind across much of the area through \nat least mid-week, with the potential for a trough to amplify across \nthe region late-week. Low level thicknesses will increase through \nthe middle of the week, maxing out ~1430 m on Mon to potentially \n1440+ m for Wed-Fri. Temperatures should moderate through mid-week, \nwith highs in the mid/upr 80s on Sun, increasing to mid/upr 90s for \nWed-Fri (10+ degrees above normal). Some locations could reach 100 \ndegrees Wed and Thu. Lows in the 70s expected through much of the \nweek. Heat index values of 100-105 are possible Tue-Thu across much \nof central NC, apart from the NW Piedmont. Given the prolonged \nperiod of heat with little relief overnight, the WPC Experimental \nHeat Risk continues to show Major to Extreme risk of heat-related \nimpacts across central NC, especially Wed and Thu. Heat advisories \nmay be needed should the forecast hold.",
